  "changes": [
    "Add two set_value_with_assignment methods with a mutable borrowed donor and FnOnce assignment callback; neither Clone nor Default is required.",
    "Use a shared assignment engine after validating the target. The target object remains in place and the caller retains the donor before and after success or assignment panic.",
    "Preserve early and late assignment failure side effects without manufacturing temporary objects or destroying either receiver or donor during the call.",
    "Support borrowed arrays whose donor occupies a disjoint tail of the same backing allocation via ordinary split mutable slices; strict Miri checks this provenance.",
    "Document existing set_value methods as Rust replacement policies and retain existing set_value_ref Clone assignment behavior unchanged."
  ],
  "probe_policy": "Native and Rust payloads record A move assignment, Q copy-assignment fallback and X destruction. Four assignment modes run on Array1/Array2 owned storage, borrowed storage with an external donor, and borrowed storage with a disjoint same-buffer donor. External donors are explicitly destroyed by their callers after the observed method result. Six rejected lower/upper row/column/index fixtures retain donor values and skip callbacks. Seven Rust tests consume all 52 native rows; two additional tests prove FnOnce captures and element types lacking Clone/Default.",
  "limitations": [
    "C++ rvalue overload resolution is represented by a mutable donor and explicit assignment closure. No automatic selection of arbitrary native move/copy operators or argument-expression evaluation is claimed.",
    "Donor and target must have disjoint valid exclusive borrows. Native self-move assignment, donors inside the receiver storage accessed through a separate index protocol, arbitrary overlapping wrapper aliases and retained raw pointers across exclusive borrows remain open domains.",
    "Shared read-only views detach on mutable access. Native writable const-reference aliases and callback reentry remain outside this safe interface.",
    "Array2 native SetValue directly indexes Data after rectangle validation; shortening its inherited Array1 below the indexed cell makes native access undefined. Rust returns a checked backing-storage error; this safe extension receives no native parity credit.",
    "Existing by-value set_value methods retain Rust replacement and old-target Drop semantics, rather than borrowing the donor as the C++ T&& overload does. Existing set_value_ref methods require Clone and cover a different overload.",
    "Rust panic and native exception comparisons cover defined target/donor state and element events. Panicking Drop, throwing-destructor termination, ABI, allocation policy and uninitialized native storage remain outside this slice.",
    "Original assertions are unchanged. These two partial-domain overload bindings do not declare either array class complete and add no global inventory or original declaration credit."
  ],
